  "slug": "binding-topic-5fe9",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply MD, crystallography and AlphaFold3 to resolve binding sites, selectivity and coupling in membrane transporters and disordered regions.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how binding partners and permeant ions are recognised and discriminated at defined sites in proteins, using combined simulation, structural and functional approaches. Nanobody mapping of the BasC transporter reveals a bias toward the intracellular cavity, while enhanced-sampling simulations resolve the multimodal landscape by which the FCP1 disordered region folds on binding Rap74. In SERT, Na+ occupancy of the Na2 site is reported to couple sodium to serotonin transport through TM1, TM5 and TM8 interactions, and in XPR1, phosphate selectivity arises from direct multi-ion competition in the pore. Among solute-binding proteins, three divergent members share a conserved loss of affinity for perfluorinated ligands, with locally tunable sensitivity to partial fluorination. The HIV-1 integrase study adds an evolutionary perspective, showing that drug-resistance mutations shift folding stability and catalytic geometry only marginally.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
